## Who logs in and what they can do

There are three roles on a **membership** (user ↔ company):

| Role | Can |
|------|-----|
| **Owner** | Everything, including inviting people |
| **Manager** | Everything except invites. Only managers approve/reopen days, edit the Planning board, set material norms and stock levels, and send or reject material requests. |
| **Team lead** | Read projects their teams are on; fill reports and sheet cells for their teams; record crew attendance, issues, notes and photos on those days; record material used on their sites; request material; move/comment on tasks assigned to their teams |

The active company lives in the **session** (`company_id`). Almost every API call does:

1. Require login
2. Require a membership for that session company
3. Filter querysets to that company
4. If the user is a team lead, narrow further (`scope_for_lead`) and only allow extra writes listed on the view as `lead_actions`

That is the tenancy wall. Two companies never share rows; PKs can coincide, so activity lookups also filter by company. Every Phase 1 model has tests proving a second company cannot read or write it.

Demo login (from seed): `manager@example.com` / `Manager-pass-123` on **River Co**. The seed also has a team lead (Mira) and demo data for every Phase 1 screen.

